\title{A Greedy Integer Sequence with Global Pair-Sum Avoidance}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 22, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a repaired greedy recurrence designed to express global additive avoidance without ambiguity. Beginning with $a_0=1$, the successor $a_{n+1}$ is the least integer strictly larger than $a_n$ that is not a sum of two values among $a_0,\ldots,a_n$, with repeated summands permitted. We prove that the successor exists after every finite nondecreasing history and satisfies $a_{n+1}\le 2a_n+1$. Every resulting trajectory is strictly increasing and contains no chronological additive triple: if $i<k$ and $j<k$, then $a_i+a_j\ne a_k$. Equivalently, every finite chronological additive hypergraph is empty. We also show that the displayed list $1,1,2,4,7,\ldots$ is incompatible with the rule. Algorithms, applications, limitations, and conjectural extensions are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A greedy recurrence chooses the least currently feasible object. In an additive setting, however, ``feasible'' must specify the candidate universe, the historical scope of the exclusions, and whether repeated summands are allowed. A rule excluding one selected sum is not the same as a rule excluding the complete pair sumset of the history. Moreover, without a requirement of forward motion, a purported sequence may simply repeat its smallest value.

We impose three explicit conditions. The next term must exceed the current term; every sum of two values already present is forbidden; and the least remaining candidate is chosen. Our first concern is well-posedness. Every forbidden sum is at most twice the current maximum, so one more than that maximum sum is always available. This witness proves both indefinite continuation and a quantitative one-step bound.

The construction prevents every later value from being a sum of two earlier values. Encoding such equations as hyperedges produces an empty chronological additive hypergraph. The same framework also diagnoses a proposed triangular prefix: $1,1,2,4,7,\ldots$ violates strict growth and contains $1+1=2$.

\section{Definitions}

We write $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and use zero-based indices.

\begin{definition}[Prior pair-sum set]
For a sequence $a:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ and $n\ge0$, let
\[
P_n(a)=\{a_i+a_j:0\le i\le n,\ 0\le j\le n\}.
\]
The indices may coincide, so every $2a_i$ with $i\le n$ is included.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Witness characterization]
An integer $s$ belongs to $P_n(a)$ if and only if there are indices $i,j\le n$ such that $a_i+a_j=s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is immediate by unpacking the image of the finite index square $\{0,\ldots,n\}^2$ under $(i,j)\mapsto a_i+a_j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Admissibility and greedy successor]
An integer $z$ is admissible after time $n$ if $a_n<z$ and $z\notin P_n(a)$. It is the greedy successor if it is admissible and no larger than any other admissible integer.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Repaired trajectory]
A globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy trajectory is a sequence with $a_0=1$ such that $a_{n+1}$ is the greedy successor after time $n$ for every $n\ge0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chronological additive triple]
For a cutoff $N$, a triple $(i,j,k)$ with $0\le i,j,k<N$ is chronological and additive if
\[
i<k,\qquad j<k,\qquad a_i+a_j=a_k.
\]
The collection of such triples is the edge set of the chronological additive hypergraph. No relation between $i$ and $j$ is required, and $i=j$ is allowed.
\end{definition}

Chronology records the information available when $a_k$ was selected. It avoids making a stronger, unordered assertion than the recurrence supplies.

\section{Existence of successors}

\begin{lemma}[Explicit escape candidate]
Suppose a finite history satisfies $a_i\le a_n$ for every $i\le n$. Then $2a_n+1$ is admissible after time $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Certainly $a_n<2a_n+1$. If $2a_n+1$ belonged to $P_n(a)$, there would be $i,j\le n$ with $a_i+a_j=2a_n+1$. But $a_i,a_j\le a_n$, whence $a_i+a_j\le2a_n$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence and boundedness of a greedy successor]
Every finite history satisfying $a_i\le a_n$ for all $i\le n$ has a greedy successor $z$, and
\[
z\le2a_n+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The escape-candidate lemma makes the admissible set nonempty. By well-ordering, it has a least element $z$. This is the greedy successor. Since $2a_n+1$ is admissible, minimality gives the bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Indefinite continuation and uniqueness]
Starting from any finite nondecreasing positive history, the greedy construction can always take another step. In particular, $a_0=1$ determines a unique infinite trajectory.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The theorem supplies a successor, and admissibility makes it larger than the old endpoint. Thus the extended history remains nondecreasing. Induction gives continuation at all times; least elements are unique.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural results}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]
Every globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy trajectory is strictly increasing: $i<j$ implies $a_i<a_j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Admissibility gives $a_n<a_{n+1}$ at each step. Transitivity gives the general claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chronological additive avoidance]
If $a$ is a globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy trajectory and $i<k$, $j<k$, then
\[
a_i+a_j\ne a_k.
\]
This includes the case $i=j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There is nothing to prove for $k=0$. Otherwise write $k=n+1$. Then $i,j\le n$, so $a_i+a_j\in P_n(a)$. The successor $a_{n+1}$ was chosen outside that set.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform one-step growth ceiling]
Every globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy trajectory satisfies
\[
a_{n+1}\le2a_n+1
\]
for all $n\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity gives $a_i\le a_n$ for every $i\le n$. The explicit escape candidate is therefore admissible, and the greedy choice is no larger.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Iterated upper bound]
For the trajectory beginning at $1$,
\[
a_n\le2^{n+1}-1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. The base case is equality. If the claim holds at $n$, then
\[
a_{n+1}\le2a_n+1\le2(2^{n+1}-1)+1=2^{n+2}-1.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Empty chronological additive hypergraph]
For every trajectory and every cutoff $N$, the chronological additive hypergraph on $0,\ldots,N-1$ has no edges.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An edge would provide $i<k$, $j<k$, and $a_i+a_j=a_k$, contradicting chronological additive avoidance.
\end{proof}

Consequently every finite chronological edge count, and every normalized edge density with positive denominator, equals zero.

\section{The incompatible triangular prefix}

Consider
\[
b_n=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2,
\]
whose initial values are $1,1,2,4,7,11,\ldots$. Its quadratic leading coefficient is $1/2$.

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the displayed triangular sequence]
The sequence $b_n=1+n(n-1)/2$ is not a globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy trajectory.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict growth fails because $b_0=b_1=1$. Independently, $b_2=2=b_0+b_1$, so the third displayed value is a forbidden prior pair sum.
\end{proof}

Thus the prefix and the global avoidance rule describe different objects. Any recurrence intended to reproduce the prefix must alter strict growth, the scope of the forbidden sums, or the convention on repeated values and summands.

\section{Algorithms and numerical behavior}

A direct generator maintains the history, forms all pair sums, and scans upward from one more than the current value until it finds an integer outside the sumset. With $m$ current terms, rebuilding the sumset takes $O(m^2)$ additions and $O(m^2)$ space. Rebuilding at every stage gives $O(N^3)$ arithmetic work through $N$ terms.

An incremental algorithm is preferable. When a new term $z$ is appended, only the sums $z+x$ with existing values $x$, together with $z+z$, are new. This requires $O(m)$ insertions at stage $m$, hence $O(N^2)$ insertions and $O(N^2)$ space through $N$ terms. The growth-ceiling theorem guarantees termination of every candidate scan.

A finite prefix certificate checks the initial value, strict growth, exclusion of the chosen successor from the current pair sumset, and membership of every skipped candidate in that sumset. The last clause certifies greedy minimality.

For arbitrary data, chronological additive triples can be enumerated directly. The number of ordered index triples inspected is
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}k^2=\frac{(N-1)N(2N-1)}6,
\]
so direct enumeration takes $\Theta(N^3)$ time. A valid trajectory yields no triples.

Generation from $1$ produces
\[
1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,\ldots.
\]
This suggests $a_n=2n+1$. If all previous values are odd, their pair sums are even. The candidate $a_n+1$ is forbidden because it equals $1+a_n$, while $a_n+2$ is odd and cannot be a sum of two earlier odd values. This parity mechanism suggests a short induction, but we keep exact classification distinct from the general theorems proved above.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The construction belongs to additive combinatorics because the evolving restricted sumset constrains each new point. It is not a Sidon condition: Sidon sets control repeated representations of sums, whereas this rule prevents a selected value from being a prior pair sum. The adjective chronological should not be omitted without an additional argument.

The existence proof exemplifies a reusable greedy pattern: bound every obstruction by an invariant, exhibit a feasible point beyond that bound, use well-ordering to select the least feasible point, and compare the greedy output with the witness.

The hypergraph translation opens a stability question. Exact greediness gives zero edges. A bounded perturbation may create some additive triples while retaining zero limiting density. Residue classes offer another bridge: the observed odd trajectory is supported on one class modulo $2$, and its pair sums occupy the other. Stabilized residue support in finite cyclic groups may govern eventual arithmetic progressions.

The analysis also illustrates sound specification design. One must say whether candidates must be new or increasing, whether all prior pairs are forbidden, whether repeated indices are allowed, and what ambient universe is used for a complement or density. The complement of the selected values in the positive integers is not the same as the time-dependent complement of $P_n(a)$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact classification]
The trajectory beginning at one is the odd sequence $a_n=2n+1$.
\end{conjecture}
The parity mechanism above isolates the likely induction step.

\begin{conjecture}[Changing the initial value]
For every positive initial value, the globally additive-avoiding greedy trajectory is eventually an arithmetic progression, with common difference determined by the additive semigroup generated by an initial segment.
\end{conjecture}
The successor bound confines choices to a short moving interval and makes residue stabilization testable.

\begin{conjecture}[Higher-order avoidance]
If candidates avoid sums of exactly $r$ prior terms, with repetition allowed, then the trajectory from one is eventually periodic modulo $r$ and has linear growth.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Perturbative hypergraph sparsity]
Any increasing sequence whose successor differs from the globally greedy admissible choice by a uniformly bounded amount has a chronological additive hypergraph with zero upper edge density.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Counting-function dichotomy]
A globally pair-sum-avoiding greedy sequence has either positive asymptotic density or a counting function bounded above by a constant multiple of the square root of the ambient cutoff, according to whether its stabilized residue support is sum-free in a finite cyclic group.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

Beginning at $1$, moving strictly forward, excluding every prior pair sum, and taking the least remaining integer defines a coherent infinite sequence. The candidate $2a_n+1$ proves that every stage can continue and yields the growth ceiling $a_{n+1}\le2a_n+1$. The trajectory is strictly increasing, contains no chronological additive triple, and therefore has an empty finite chronological additive hypergraph. The triangular list $1,1,2,4,7,\ldots$ fails these requirements immediately. With the specification repaired, exact classification, modular stabilization, higher-order avoidance, and perturbative sparsity become precise research problems.

\end{document}

